---
/**
 * Custom events for Vercel Web Analytics.
 *
 * One delegated listener for the whole site, driven by attributes: `data-va`
 * names an event that fires on click, `data-va-view` names one that fires when
 * the element first reaches the viewport, and any `data-va-*` alongside them
 * becomes a property — `data-va-briefed="no"` becomes `{ briefed: 'no' }`.
 * Adding an event is then a markup change on the element it describes, which
 * is the only way a site that otherwise ships no JavaScript can afford to have
 * events at all.
 *
 * Two properties per event, and no more: that is the Pro plan's ceiling (the
 * Web Analytics Plus add-on raises it to eight). Names, keys and values cap at
 * 255 characters each, and a value has to be a string, number, boolean or
 * null — a nested object is dropped.
 *
 * Nothing here records what the URL already says. The page view carries the
 * path, so an event is only worth firing for the dimension the path is missing
 * — whether the chapter behind a tile has a brief, which translation someone
 * left for. Every event is also billed like a page view, so none of them fire
 * on a timer or on scroll, and a view event fires once per page load rather
 * than every time the element scrolls back in.
 *
 * Running in the browser is the point rather than a limitation. This site is
 * server-rendered and most of what requests it is a crawler; the beacon only
 * runs where there is a JavaScript engine, so these counts are people. A
 * server-side `track()` could not tell the two apart.
 *
 * `window.va` is the queue shim the Vercel adapter injects into <head> — see
 * `webAnalytics` in astro.config.mjs. It buffers anything fired before the
 * real script finishes loading, so nothing below has to wait on it, and
 * turning web analytics off removes the shim and stops all of this quietly.
 *
 * The explanation lives up here rather than in the script because an inline
 * script is served to every reader on every page, and these are the same
 * bytes 31,000 verse hubs would each carry. Same reason as the comment in
 * Header.astro. What is left below is deliberately bare:
 *
 * - the listener is delegated because these events sit on links rendered in
 *	 loops, and binding one each would mean one listener per chapter tile;
 * - `closest` catches a click that landed on something inside the link;
 * - `data-va-view` is excluded from the properties because it names the event
 *	 rather than describing it.
 */
---

<script is:inline>
	(() => {
		const va = window.va;
		if (typeof va !== 'function') {
			return;
		}

		const send = (el, name) => {
			if (!name) {
				return;
			}
			const data = {};
			for (const attr of el.attributes) {
				if (attr.name.startsWith('data-va-') && attr.name !== 'data-va-view') {
					data[attr.name.slice('data-va-'.length)] = attr.value;
				}
			}
			va('event', { name, data });
		};

		document.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
			const target = event.target;
			const el = target instanceof Element ? target.closest('[data-va]') : null;
			if (el) {
				send(el, el.getAttribute('data-va'));
			}
		});

		const observed = document.querySelectorAll('[data-va-view]');
		if (observed.length && 'IntersectionObserver' in window) {
			const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
				for (const entry of entries) {
					if (entry.isIntersecting) {
						observer.unobserve(entry.target);
						send(entry.target, entry.target.getAttribute('data-va-view'));
					}
				}
			});
			for (const el of observed) {
				observer.observe(el);
			}
		}
	})();
</script>
